# Always fucking run tests

Do not ask first. The repo `AGENTS.md` says to ask before running tests on your
own; this workspace overrides that. Code changed -> tests run -> then you may
say done. A green you did not produce is not a green.

## Shared lock: 6 slots under `/tmp/test-lock`

This box is shared. Builds and tests take a slot. At most ~6 concurrent
holders; otherwise wait until a slot drops. Release when the command exits
(including failure). `flock`, not mkdir -- a dead process drops the lock.

Exception: `rebase_update.sh` in compile-only mode (its default, and
what `rebase_update_batch.sh` uses) deliberately runs unslotted -- it
runs no tests, so it does not queue behind the runs that do.
`--full-test` takes a slot like everything else.

    bash ~/bin/with-test-lock -- <build-or-test-command>
    bash ~/bin/with-test-lock --status
    bash ~/bin/with-test-lock --free     # free-slot count

Do not acquire in one shell and run in another; the wrapper is the holder.
Backgrounding is fine if the wrapper is the parent (`block_until_ms: 0` /
`run_in_background` around the wrapper).

If the helper is missing, the protocol is six flock files
`/tmp/test-lock/slot-{0,1,2,3,4,5}` -- non-blocking acquire, else wait, then run,
then drop the fd.

## Scala builds. Drift is real.

Python tests against a stale jar are a lie. Before PySpark tests:
`build/sbt -Phive package` (same as after merging master). If you touched
Scala/Java, also run the matching `testOnly` suites -- Python-only is not
coverage for the JVM side.

    bash ~/bin/with-test-lock -- build/sbt -Phive package
    bash ~/bin/with-test-lock -- build/sbt -Phive 'sql/testOnly *MySuite'

## Compile+test with retries: `spark-compile-test-and-retry`

`~/bin/spark-compile-test-and-retry` from the worktree root is
the build+test engine (formerly `spark-preflight`). It plans from the
branch's own `dev/sparktestsupport/modules.py` (CI's module graph and `-P`
profiles, right on every branch), rebuilds (`build/sbt <profiles> package`,
plus `sql/Test/compile` when PySpark tests run), runs the suites to
completion under the test lock, then re-runs each failed test individually
so flakes and real failures are not reported as the same thing. One sbt at
a time: whole-module runs share ONE `sbt all a/test b/test ...` (modules run
concurrently, up to SparkBuild's 4 test JVMs; logs `<module>-tests.log`),
diff-derived suites share one boot per module
(`<module>/batched-N-suites.log`), and scala retries batch per module too.
PySpark modules spread over up to `--jobs` lanes (default 3): lanes past the
first only start a test while the lock has 2+ free slots, and each test
takes and drops its own slot. Logs and `summary.txt` land in
`target/compile-test-and-retry-logs/<timestamp>/`. Exit 1 = real failures,
fix before pushing. Exit 0 with flakes listed = pushable, but read the
flakes.

Timing: the run that counts is the one AFTER your changes are in. A run at
the start is fine as a baseline to see what's already broken (and makes
later failures easier to attribute), but a baseline green says nothing
about your changes -- the after-run is still required.

Four speeds: `--fast` is the diff-derived suites only, `--modules` is
every suite in every touched module (hours), the default (no flag) is what
PR CI runs -- the touched modules plus every module depending on one, full
suites, with a core or build change mapping to everything (hours to days) --
and `--everything` is every SBT module and every PySpark goal (days). On a
python-only diff the default and `--modules` skip PySpark test modules that
import nothing the diff touched (Spark's grimp check; needs `grimp` in the
venv) -- less than PR CI from a fork runs. Explicit suite specs override the
speed, but only on spark-compile-test-and-retry.

**Choosing the speed -- make the call, do not ask.** Default to `--fast`.
Step up to `--modules` when the change is more complex (cross-module, SQL
core, streaming, anything that plausibly breaks neighbours). Run
`--everything` only when the change is super complex AND Holden has not
said she is in a rush. Do not block on prompting her to pick a speed; judge,
proceed, and say which speed you chose and why.

Machine busyness shapes the plan:

- **Not busy** (no other PRs in flight, test slots free -- check
  `~/bin/with-test-lock --status` and `gh pr list --author @me --state
  open`): run `spark-presend --fast`, push, open the draft PR, then run
  `spark-presend` (no flag: what PR CI runs) in the background and fix any
  follow-on failures it surfaces. The fast gate gets the branch up; the
  CI-scope run catches the rest without blocking the opening.
- **Busy** (multiple PRs in flight or slots contended): `--fast` or
  `--modules` alone -- do not pile an hours-long CI-scope run onto a
  contended box. Note that it is still owed and say so.

It is long. Background it (`block_until_ms: 0` / `run_in_background`) and
read the log; do not sit in a 30s foreground shell.

- **Before pushing a Spark branch: run `spark-presend` (it calls this).**
  Not optional.
- **When you think a Spark task is done and code changed: run
  `spark-presend`** (speed per the policy above) before declaring done --
  do not just suggest it. Pure doc changes (`docs/`, `*.md`, `*.rst`) are
  exempt -- both scripts no-op on a doc-only diff.
- `--skip-build` only when the jar is known current (you just built it).
  `--dry-run` prints the plan. `--base <ref>` for release branches.
- Explicit suites go to the test engine, not the gate: `spark-presend` takes no
 positional argument and errors out on one. Run
 `~/bin/spark-compile-test-and-retry '*RocksDBSuite*'` instead.
- The **test phase** moves `JAVA_HOME` off a nix JDK onto a system one, and
 refuses to pick a JDK Spark rejects. `spark-presend` itself does not: its lint
 phase runs under whatever `JAVA_HOME` you gave it.
- It sets the RocksDB-relevant env itself (AWS vars genuinely unset,
 `SPARK_LOCAL_IP/HOSTNAME`, PySpark isolated per test the way
 `python/run-tests` does it -- own `TMPDIR`, `java.io.tmpdir`, warehouse and
 `SPARK_LOCAL_DIRS` on the worktree disk -- an exec-check on `target/` where
 every test JVM extracts the JNI libs, nofile bump) and
 auto-enables `RUN_HYPOTHESIS=1` (200 examples) for the gated transpile
 suite wherever it gets enumerated.

## Pre-send: `spark-presend`

`~/bin/spark-presend` from the worktree root is THE gate before
opening/updating a PR -- use it for everything. It auto-detects the base
branch (open PR via gh, else the branch name, else master; `--base`
overrides), then runs: static checks (conf `.version()` vs the base
branch's real version -- the SPARK-52611 class, `.withBindingPolicy`
required on new confs where the base has the API (4.2+) and an ERROR if a
backport adds it on an older branch -- drop the call and the import there,
dup conf definitions, license headers, commit-subject hygiene, leftover
debug calls), the repo linters for touched languages plus
`dev/check-license` (concurrent and lock-free -- linters are cheap; the lock
bounds test JVM load) with the test build running alongside, then
`spark-compile-test-and-retry` (failed tests retried individually). A 24h
wall-clock cap kills a wedged run, child tree included. The
test phase speed follows the policy above (`--fast` by default, `--modules`
for complex changes, no flag for what PR CI runs, `--everything` only when
super complex and not in a rush). `--dry-run` does the static checks only,
`--mima` adds dev/mima (after the build: it compiles too).
`~/bin/rebase_update.sh` uses the same base detection and force-pushes to
the fork (`origin`), never upstream.
